import { mkdir, readFile, rename, writeFile } from 'node:fs/promises';
import { dirname } from 'node:path';

export class BookmarkStore {
  constructor(file) {
    this.file = file;
    this.bookmarks = [];
    this.ready = false;
    this.writeQueue = Promise.resolve();
  }

  async load() {
    if (this.ready) return this;
    await mkdir(dirname(this.file), { recursive: true });
    try {
      const parsed = JSON.parse(await readFile(this.file, 'utf8'));
      this.bookmarks = Array.isArray(parsed.bookmarks) ? parsed.bookmarks : [];
    } catch (error) {
      if (error.code !== 'ENOENT') throw error;
      this.bookmarks = [];
      await this.persist();
    }
    this.ready = true;
    return this;
  }

  list() {
    return this.bookmarks.map(item => structuredClone(item));
  }

  get(id) {
    const item = this.bookmarks.find(bookmark => bookmark.id === id);
    return item ? structuredClone(item) : null;
  }

  async insert(bookmark) {
    this.bookmarks.unshift(structuredClone(bookmark));
    await this.persist();
    return this.get(bookmark.id);
  }

  async replace(id, bookmark) {
    const index = this.bookmarks.findIndex(item => item.id === id);
    if (index < 0) return null;
    this.bookmarks[index] = structuredClone(bookmark);
    await this.persist();
    return this.get(id);
  }

  async remove(id) {
    const index = this.bookmarks.findIndex(item => item.id === id);
    if (index < 0) return null;
    const [removed] = this.bookmarks.splice(index, 1);
    await this.persist();
    return structuredClone(removed);
  }

  async clear() {
    this.bookmarks = [];
    await this.persist();
  }

  async persist() {
    const payload = JSON.stringify({ version: 1, bookmarks: this.bookmarks }, null, 2);
    const temp = `${this.file}.tmp`;
    this.writeQueue = this.writeQueue.then(async () => {
      await writeFile(temp, payload, 'utf8');
      await rename(temp, this.file);
    });
    await this.writeQueue;
  }
}
# Design decisions

## Architecture

- A dependency-light Node.js HTTP service owns persistence, page capture, URL
  normalization, and bookmark operations.
- A browser client renders the library and performs the approved interactions.
- Bookmark data is persisted as JSON with atomic file replacement. This keeps
  the first-cycle application portable while isolating persistence behind a
  store class for later replacement.
- Phase 1 prototype files are not imported, copied, or referenced by the
  production application.

## Page capture

- The service fetches a page once for metadata and readable text. It extracts
  standard title, description, icon, and preview metadata, then retains readable
  article-like text as the saved copy.
- A pending copy can be retried explicitly and by a background retry loop.
- A successful later capture fills the archive and missing visual metadata but
  never replaces person-entered title, description, or notes.

## Identity and consistency

- Bookmark identity uses a canonical address: fragments and known referral
  parameters are removed, default ports are normalized, and a trailing slash is
  normalized. Other query values remain meaningful.
- Tags are compared case-insensitively while preserving the first spelling.

## Reading behavior

- The client opens a blank new tab immediately, asks the service whether the
  original is reachable, then directs that tab to either the original address
  or the local saved-copy reader. The library tab never navigates away.

## Safety

- Deletion is available only inside Edit and always requires a named
  confirmation. Cancelling the confirmation does not submit or clear the form.
- User-originated strings are escaped before browser rendering. Saved-copy text
  is stored and rendered as text rather than executable page markup.

## Deliberately deferred

- Accounts, sharing, cross-device synchronization, browser extensions, import
  and export, and multi-user permissions were not part of the approved cycle.
Scenario ID: SCN-009
Title: Revisit a saved readable copy when the original page disappears
Type: Edge Case
Approval status: approved
Approval session: SESSION-009
Approval cycle: 1

## Approved behaviour
The app captures a readable copy of an article when the bookmark is first saved. If the original page later becomes unavailable, the bookmark card clearly identifies that the original cannot be reached and that a saved copy is ready. Clicking the card through the usual reading action automatically opens the saved copy in a new browser tab while preserving the library in its original tab. The saved copy contains the title, description, and readable article body rather than only metadata.

## Gherkin
Feature: Preserve saved content

  Scenario: Capture a readable copy when saving
    Given the person is saving an available article page
    When the bookmark is saved
    Then the app captures a readable copy containing the article title, description, and body

  Scenario: Open a saved copy after the original disappears
    Given the library contains a bookmark with a captured readable copy
    And the original page can no longer be reached
    When the person views the bookmark
    Then the card states that the original page is unavailable
    And the card states that a saved copy is ready
    When the person clicks the bookmark card
    Then the saved readable copy opens in a new browser tab
    And the library remains in place in the original tab

## Assumptions
- Readable copies are captured automatically when a bookmark is first saved.
- A readable copy includes the article body, not just bookmark metadata.
- The app can identify that an original page is unavailable.
- The usual card-click reading action automatically falls back to the saved copy.
- The fallback copy opens in a new tab.

## Related scenarios
- SCN-001
- SCN-005
Scenario ID: SCN-013
Title: Keep long bookmark content complete while cards remain scannable
Type: Edge Case
Approval status: approved
Approval session: SESSION-013
Approval cycle: 1

## Approved behaviour
When a bookmark has an unusually long title, description, personal note, and many tags, the library card shortens the long text previews with an ellipsis and wraps tags cleanly so the overview remains tidy. Opening Edit bookmark presents the full uncut title, description, and note for reading and editing. Display shortening never discards the person's content.

## Gherkin
Feature: Display long bookmark content

  Scenario: Show long content on a library card
    Given a bookmark has a very long title, description, personal note, and several tags
    When the person views it in the library
    Then the card shows compact shortened previews of the long text
    And the card wraps the tags cleanly
    And the card remains scannable

  Scenario: Edit the full long content
    Given long bookmark text is shortened on its library card
    When the person opens Edit bookmark
    Then the full title is present and editable
    And the full description is present and editable
    And the full personal note is present and editable

## Assumptions
- Text is shortened only for the library-card presentation.
- Shortening is visibly indicated with an ellipsis.
- The stored content is never truncated.

## Related scenarios
- SCN-004
Scenario ID: SCN-015
Title: Cancel deletion without changing the bookmark
Type: Edge Case
Approval status: approved
Approval session: SESSION-015
Approval cycle: 1

## Approved behaviour
When the named delete confirmation is open and the person chooses Keep bookmark, the warning closes and the unchanged edit form returns. The bookmark and all of its details remain intact; cancellation has no deletion or editing side effects.

## Gherkin
Feature: Delete a bookmark

  Scenario: Cancel bookmark deletion
    Given the named delete confirmation is open for a bookmark
    When the person chooses Keep bookmark
    Then the delete confirmation closes
    And the unchanged bookmark edit form is shown
    And the bookmark remains in the library with all of its details intact

## Assumptions
- Keep bookmark is the non-destructive action in the delete confirmation.
- Cancelling deletion does not discard unsaved form content or modify stored bookmark content.

## Related scenarios
- SCN-008

